00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:15.604
- Study the business,
over to you, Ross Perot!

00:00:15.604 --> 00:00:17.807
(rooster crowing)

00:00:17.807 --> 00:00:20.373
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:20.373 --> 00:00:23.113
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:00:23.113 --> 00:00:27.008
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:27.008 --> 00:00:29.150
- My advice to a person
who's considering starting

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.871
a small business, is number one,

00:00:31.871 --> 00:00:33.664
serve an apprenticeship in an industry

00:00:33.664 --> 00:00:36.563
you're really excited about.

00:00:36.563 --> 00:00:38.254
Let's assume you have a Masters Degree

00:00:38.254 --> 00:00:39.843
in Business from Harvard.

00:00:39.843 --> 00:00:42.206
Go to work second and third
shift on a factory floor.

00:00:42.206 --> 00:00:43.476
Learn it from the bottom up.

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:45.443
Really know that business.

00:00:45.443 --> 00:00:50.129
Work there a few years,
insist on doing every job,

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:52.828
and one of these days, a light
is going on in your head,

00:00:52.828 --> 00:00:55.141
and you're going to have
a really unorthodox idea,

00:00:55.141 --> 00:00:56.670
an unmet need.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.230
The way you find it,
is by burying yourself

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:00.753
in an industry you adore.

00:01:00.753 --> 00:01:03.559
That's the way you
identify the unmet need,

00:01:03.559 --> 00:01:05.988
and then, you have the germ of the idea

00:01:05.988 --> 00:01:07.431
to start your own company.

00:01:07.431 --> 00:01:09.001
- So what do you think?

00:01:09.001 --> 00:01:11.285
- You know what, before
I got into the career

00:01:11.285 --> 00:01:13.086
of dancing, running the dance school,

00:01:13.086 --> 00:01:14.622
I wanted to be a marriage counselor,

00:01:14.622 --> 00:01:17.802
so I went into psychology, I
started getting my education,

00:01:17.802 --> 00:01:19.642
by working on my bachelor's.

00:01:19.642 --> 00:01:21.804
Through it, I realized
that it wasn't for me,

00:01:21.804 --> 00:01:23.124
then I changed career paths,

00:01:23.124 --> 00:01:24.673
I wanted to be a police officer.

00:01:24.673 --> 00:01:27.123
I became a security guard
to see what the night life,

00:01:27.123 --> 00:01:28.993
night shift life, dealing with people

00:01:28.993 --> 00:01:30.973
who are not necessarily always happy.

00:01:30.973 --> 00:01:32.020
How was that?

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:33.304
I realized that, that wasn't for me,

00:01:33.304 --> 00:01:35.191
and each of these career
choices, I went into the field

00:01:35.191 --> 00:01:38.582
to see what it's like to
experience some part of it

00:01:38.582 --> 00:01:40.477
and figure out if it's right for me.

00:01:40.477 --> 00:01:42.885
With TDS, Toronto Dance
Salsa, it was the same thing.

00:01:42.885 --> 00:01:44.158
I started as a student.

00:01:44.158 --> 00:01:45.653
I went to become a helper-assistant.

00:01:45.653 --> 00:01:48.143
I became an instructor, and
when I had the opportunity

00:01:48.143 --> 00:01:50.478
to run, basically the business,

00:01:50.478 --> 00:01:51.726
I realized that it was for me,

00:01:51.726 --> 00:01:53.272
because of all of the
experience that I had

00:01:53.272 --> 00:01:55.660
to see what it's like and the environment,

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:56.757
and I love the environment,

00:01:56.757 --> 00:01:59.136
so why would I want to be somewhere else?

00:01:59.136 --> 00:02:00.400
So, absolutely, I connect with them.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.821
If you want to go into
a different industry,

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:03.276
if you want to start your own business,

00:02:03.276 --> 00:02:04.750
see what it's like, see
what kind of experience

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.919
you can get, work for someone else.

00:02:06.919 --> 00:02:09.507
If you want to be a lawyer,
and you want to open up

00:02:09.507 --> 00:02:12.051
your own business, work
for a law firm, figure out

00:02:12.051 --> 00:02:14.399
if that's the kind of
environment you want to have,

00:02:14.399 --> 00:02:15.455
and then from there, you can figure out

00:02:15.455 --> 00:02:17.063
do you want to open up your own law firm.

00:02:17.063 --> 00:02:18.613
- Yeah, I've always
been a big believer that

00:02:18.613 --> 00:02:20.733
you shouldn't just jump
full in on business.

00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:23.535
Go work for somebody who's
done what you want to do.

00:02:23.535 --> 00:02:25.721
Work for a company that
has 10 employees, or less,

00:02:25.721 --> 00:02:27.516
in the industry that you want to be in,

00:02:27.516 --> 00:02:28.595
and go work for them.

00:02:28.595 --> 00:02:30.122
You're getting paid to learn.

00:02:30.122 --> 00:02:31.275
You're getting paid to make mistakes.

00:02:31.275 --> 00:02:34.072
You're getting paid to get
connections in the industry.

00:02:34.072 --> 00:02:35.454
Normally, you pay to go to school.

00:02:35.454 --> 00:02:37.630
Here you're getting paid to do it,

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.856
and you're going to make all the mistakes

00:02:39.856 --> 00:02:41.845
on somebody else's dime,
and then when you're ready

00:02:41.845 --> 00:02:43.762
to start your own company,
you have the connections,

00:02:43.762 --> 00:02:44.876
you have the experience.

00:02:44.876 --> 00:02:47.358
One of the reasons why I know so much more

00:02:47.358 --> 00:02:50.500
about entrepreneurship, is
after I sold my first business,

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.010
I only had one company, it was
a biotech software company.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.838
When I sold it, I joined
a venture capital company,

00:02:55.838 --> 00:02:58.105
and it was these guys who
were in their 60s or 70s,

00:02:58.105 --> 00:03:01.192
and I was this young
punk kid joining them,

00:03:01.192 --> 00:03:02.899
and they taught me a lot about

00:03:02.899 --> 00:03:04.885
all sorts of different businesses.

00:03:04.885 --> 00:03:06.916
You know, everyday, we had
new companies comin' in,

00:03:06.916 --> 00:03:09.952
and I was able to kind of
leech off of their decades

00:03:09.952 --> 00:03:12.392
of collective experience,
to learn how different

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:15.198
industries are run, and it
got me there so much faster

00:03:15.198 --> 00:03:17.611
than if I was just tryin'
to do everything myself.

00:03:17.611 --> 00:03:20.086
So, I love the idea, if
you're just starting up,

00:03:20.086 --> 00:03:21.082
and you want to get into businesses,

00:03:21.082 --> 00:03:22.370
you can start your business on the side,

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.945
but go work for a company that
you respect and look up to,

00:03:24.945 --> 00:03:26.808
that you want to be like
in a couple of years,

00:03:26.808 --> 00:03:29.325
learn as much as you can
from them and when you have

00:03:29.325 --> 00:03:31.135
enough money from your own thing,

00:03:31.135 --> 00:03:32.546
that you can afford to quit your job

00:03:32.546 --> 00:03:35.160
and go do that full-time,
that's when you make the leap.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.117
So the question of the day, today, is?

00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:38.932
- Would you be willing to take a pay cut

00:03:38.932 --> 00:03:41.444
to work for someone who's
the expert in the industry

00:03:41.444 --> 00:03:44.477
that you're thinking of
breaking into, and why?

00:03:44.477 --> 00:03:45.310
- I like it.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:46.149
Leave it in the comments below,

00:03:46.149 --> 00:03:47.822
